# Summary

When designing a large-scale system, we need to think beyond individual classes and functions.

```text
                    ┌─────────────────────┐
                    │     REQUIREMENTS    │
                    │                     │
                    │ What should we      │
                    │ build?              │
                    └──────────┬──────────┘
                               │
                               ▼
                    ┌─────────────────────┐
                    │    ARCHITECTURE     │
                    │                     │
                    │ How should the      │
                    │ system be structured│
                    │ and organized?      │
                    └──────────┬──────────┘
                               │
                               ▼
                    ┌─────────────────────┐
                    │       DESIGN        │
                    │                     │
                    │ How should the      │
                    │ components work?    │
                    └──────────┬──────────┘
                               │
                               ▼
                    ┌─────────────────────┐
                    │   IMPLEMENTATION    │
                    │                     │
                    │ How do we actually  │
                    │ write the code?     │
                    └─────────────────────┘
```

The central idea is:

> **The larger the system, the higher the level of abstraction we need to think about.**

System Design is therefore not simply about writing more code. It is about understanding **requirements, constraints, trade-offs, system structure, and how individual components work together to form a reliable system.**
